(*
porta space-sync — the bridge publishes its own VM internals. #56 capability (3):
"metrics die with the node", and /stats is a PULL, which is a human affordance —
an agent arrives after the corpse is cold.

Published by the BRIDGE and not by each app (design D4): the bridge is HANDED `space`
as a constructor capability, so it does not conjure space access, and it already owns
the check-in — the wake everything must ride (D7). Every bridged app then inherits
metrics without re-implementing anything, which is the duplication that gave
obs/health three inconsistent definitions.

v1 publishes on EVERY check-in. Escalating on degradation was designed and then
deliberately staged out (D6): it needed five invented constants, and D7 already
capped its worst case at exactly this — one record per check-in. The ceiling of the
elaborate design IS the simple design.

The socket is nil, so no I/O is attempted; publishMetrics is called directly, which
is the seam under test.
expect:
tag true
heapSize positive true
queueHighWater present true
pulseDrops present true
pulseStale present true
seq advanced true
*)
class MetricsOnCheckIn packageUsing: manifest = () (
  public main: platform args: args = (
    | space bridge before m spans |
    space:: platform space.
    spans:: platform Spans new.
    before:: space stamp.

    bridge:: platform PortaBridge socket: nil space: space id: 'test' peer: '::1' port: 1 spans: spans.
    bridge publishMetrics.

    m:: (space channel: 'sys/metrics') peek: '' ifAbsent: [ nil ].
    ('tag ' , (m tag = #Metrics) printString) println.
    ('heapSize positive ' , ((m at: #hsz ifAbsent: [ -1 ]) > 0) printString) println.
    ('queueHighWater present ' , ((m at: #qhw ifAbsent: [ -1 ]) >= 0) printString) println.
    ('pulseDrops present ' , ((m at: #pdr ifAbsent: [ -1 ]) >= 0) printString) println.
    (* rete#214: prim slot 7 has to reach the published cell, not merely exist in C.
       This is the whole wiring — sysStats -> Kernel>>metrics -> the sys/metrics cell
       -> the push frame — and an absent field answers -1 here, so presence is what
       is being asserted rather than a value that happens to be zero. *)
    ('pulseStale present ' , ((m at: #pst ifAbsent: [ -1 ]) >= 0) printString) println.

    (* The write must STAMP, or the cell exists locally and never rides a push:
       collectOutbound scans `changedSince: acked`, so an unstamped value is
       invisible to the very thing this record is for. *)
    ('seq advanced ' , (space stamp > before) printString) println.
  )
) : (
)
